values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i026$il$, $il$Claude writes the subject line, and chasers thread instead of starting a new email$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T08:40:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 4, '{}'::text[], array[$il$i007$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-15T13:07:24.136Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$The Subject field sits empty next to a finished body. Claude must draft the subject alongside the body, in the same card, editable and regenerable the same way.

WHERE A SUBJECT IS NEEDED, settled by Oliver on 16 September:
  Every InMail, new and chaser alike. An InMail always carries a subject, so every one needs a written subject, not just the first.
  The FIRST email only.
  Email chasers do NOT get a new subject. They continue the existing one, the way a reply does.
  DMs need no subject field at all and it should not be shown.

EMAIL REPLIES GO TO EVERYONE ON THE THREAD by default, not just the sender.

One correction worth building around rather than discovering later: writing RE or AW in front of the subject does not make an email thread. Threading is done by headers. Details below.

Note this is not only wiring. There is no subject line rule anywhere in the EA bundle, so the rule has to be written. A house pattern taken from Oliver's real sent mail is in the background.$il$,
$il$RE AND AW ARE COSMETIC. A mail client threads on the In-Reply-To and References headers, not on the subject text. If a chaser goes out as a fresh message with RE prepended, it will NOT sit in the thread in the recipient's client, it will be a separate email that merely looks like a reply, which is worse than either doing it properly or not pretending. So: send the chaser as a real reply carrying those headers, and let the subject follow.

DO NOT STACK PREFIXES. German Outlook writes AW, English writes RE, and a thread that has crossed both produces AW: RE: AW: if each side adds its own. Rule: carry the existing subject through unchanged, and if a prefix has to be added, add exactly one, matching the language already used in the thread. Never append a second.

REPLY ALL, WITH ONE CAUTION FROM OLIVER'S OWN THREAD. Default to everyone on the thread, yes. But it must not resurrect people who were deliberately dropped: on the refurbed thread Oliver moved Matteo Ferraris to BCC to save his inbox after the introduction, and added Mark to CC. A blind reply all against the original recipient list would put Matteo back in. So reply all means everyone on the LAST message, not everyone who has ever been on the thread, and the recipient list should be visible and editable before sending.

THE HOUSE PATTERN, observed across Oliver's recent sends:
  Pier Protect fuer Revendo - Erste Unterlagen und Preisstaffelung
  Pier x notebooksbilliger - Zusammenfassung und naechste Schritte
  Very x Pier, introduction via Mark Harrison-North
  Retech: where we stand and next steps
The shape is: name the two parties, then a dash, then plainly what is inside. Either Pier x Partner or Product fuer Partner. No hooks, no curiosity gaps, no questions. It tells the reader what the email contains before they open it.

RULES THAT FALL OUT OF THAT:
1. Name the partner. A subject with no company name in it is wrong.
2. Say what is inside, not why they should care. Erste Unterlagen und Preisstaffelung beats A quick idea for Revendo.
3. Where there is a route in, name it. Introduction via Mark Harrison-North does more work than any hook.
4. Match the language of the body. A German body takes a German subject.
5. On a reply, keep their subject. Never rewrite a live thread's subject.
6. Roughly 40 to 70 characters so it survives a phone inbox.
7. No exclamation marks, no em dashes, sentence case. Same rules as the body.

INMAIL SUBJECTS ARE A DIFFERENT PROBLEM FROM EMAIL SUBJECTS and the house pattern above does not transfer cleanly. An InMail subject is shown in a LinkedIn notification next to the sender's name and photo, not in a crowded inbox, and Pier x Partner reads like a mailshot there. Worth writing the InMail rule separately rather than reusing the email one, and worth testing before it is fixed in the prompt.

WORTH CHECKING BEFORE BUILDING: the email pattern is right for warm and mid thread mail, which is most of what Oliver sends. Nobody has tested it on genuine cold first touch, where a named parties subject may read as a mailshot. Ask Mark, or A/B it, rather than assuming it transfers.$il$);
