insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i028$il$, $il$Long text is cut off in fields and cannot be read$il$, $il$Lovable UI$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T09:02:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 5, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Text is cut off across the company record and cannot be read without clicking into the field. Fix it by how long the field actually gets: auto-growing textareas for short overflow, auto-grow to about ten lines then scroll plus an expand control for long prose, and wrap plus fade in table cells. Hover tooltips are a supplement, never the mechanism, because they fail on touch and on keyboard. General rule worth adopting: no field should hide content at rest.$il$,
$il$WHY NOT HOVER ONLY: it fails completely on a tablet or any touch input, it is not reachable by keyboard so it fails accessibility, the text cannot be selected or copied out of a tooltip, and a long Coverage Summary in a floating box is harder to read than in place. Fine as a supplement, wrong as the answer.

RECOMMENDED, by how long the field actually gets:

1. SHORT OVERFLOW, one to three lines. Distribution, Insurance Product Types, Insurance Provider. Make these auto-growing textareas rather than single line inputs. The field grows to fit its content on load, so nothing is ever hidden and no interaction is needed at all. This is the main fix and it covers most of the screenshot.

2. LONG PROSE. Coverage Summary, Evidence, Notes. Auto-grow to a sensible maximum, roughly 8 to 10 lines, then scroll inside the field, with a small expand control that opens it full width for reading and editing. Never a fixed two-line box.

3. TABLE CELLS, where growing is not an option. Wrap to two lines, then a text fade rather than an abrupt cut so it is visibly truncated, and the whole row opens the record on click. Add title text on hover as a supplement, not as the mechanism.

ONE GENERAL RULE WORTH ADOPTING: no field should ever hide content at rest. If it is on the screen it should be readable without an interaction. That is the efficiency point Oliver keeps making, and it is cheaper to honour at build time than to retrofit.$il$);
