insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i031$il$, $il$Message history is incomplete, and LinkedIn vs Sales Nav URLs must be two fields$il$, $il$Integrations$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T09:50:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, array[$il$i032$il$,$il$i015$il$,$il$i033$il$,$il$i042$il$,$il$i045$il$,$il$i046$il$,$il$i048$il$]::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Catherine Meng P571 is missing the 20 August message and her plain LinkedIn URL. The gap originates upstream in the lake, so this is not a Lovable import bug. Two jobs. Split LinkedIn URL from Sales Nav URL, mirroring the two columns the lake already holds, and backfill. Then make record at send time a hard rule, because LinkedIn's own export is DMs only and an unlogged InMail is gone for good. For Brad to confirm: are both InMails and DMs captured by the integration?$il$,
$il$WHAT I CHECKED IN THE SOURCE LAKE:
- Her last touch on record is 10 August, T678, a LinkedIn DM. The 20 August message Oliver sent is NOT in the lake either. So Lovable imported faithfully and this is NOT a Lovable bug. The gap originates upstream.
- Her Sales Nav URL is present. Her plain LinkedIn URL is EMPTY, exactly as Oliver found.
- Her next action was Chaser #1 due 17 August, which fits a send around the 20th that then never got logged.

THE URL GAP IS SYSTEMIC, not one contact. Across 665 contacts: 437 have a LinkedIn URL, 423 have a Sales Nav URL, 314 have both, and 119 have NEITHER. So 228 are missing the plain LinkedIn URL and 242 are missing the Sales Nav one.

ON SEPARATING THE TWO URLS: agreed, and worth knowing the lake ALREADY holds them as two distinct columns, 'LinkedIn URL' and 'LinkedIn URL (Sales Nav)'. They are different things, a /in/ profile versus a /sales/lead/ record, and only the /in/ form is usable for enrichment lookups. If Lovable has collapsed them into one field, that is the fix. Do not invent a new scheme, mirror the two that exist.

THE DEEPER PROBLEM, AND IT DOES NOT GET FIXED BY BETTER SYNCING. There is a standing rule in the engine that connection state is a cheap snapshot and reconstructable, but message history is NOT: it has to be written at the moment of sending or it is gone. The reason is that LinkedIn's own export is DMs only and contains NO InMails, ever. So if the 20 August message was an InMail, no sync, no scrape and no reconciliation will ever recover it. It exists only in Oliver's Sales Nav inbox.

That makes 'record at send time' a hard requirement of the Lovable build rather than a nicety, and it is the single most important thing about the outreach module. A missing touch is worse than no data because the guards read it: a contact with no logged touch looks like a fresh prospect, which is how someone gets messaged twice.

FOR BRAD TO CONFIRM, Oliver's question: are ALL messages, InMails and DMs both, sent through Sales Nav and captured by the integration? The standing operating rule is that Sales Nav is the default channel even for first degree connections, so the intent is yes. What needs confirming is whether the 4-hourly watcher actually captures both, or only one of them.$il$);
insert into public.improvement_comments (team_id, improvement_id, who, at, text)
select i.team_id, i.id, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-09T09:51:00.000Z'::timestamptz,
$il$Two concrete asks that fall out of this. First, a backfill: 119 contacts have neither URL and cannot be actioned or enriched at all until they do, so that is a worklist in its own right. Second, a guard: refuse to draft to a contact whose message history was last synced before a given date, rather than assuming silence means nothing was sent. Oliver has been bitten by this before, when seven outreach cards were produced on the basis of an incomplete Outreach Log and every one of them was wrong. The rule that came out of it was to prove the thread before drafting. Lovable should enforce that rather than rely on remembering it.$il$
from public.improvements i where i.team_id = (select id from public.teams limit 1) and i.item_key = $il$i031$il$;

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i032$il$, $il$Jump straight out of the review card: Sales Nav, LinkedIn, and the company website$il$, $il$Lovable UI$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-09T10:15:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 5, array[$il$i017$il$,$il$i035$il$]::text[], array[$il$i031$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$CONFIRMED FROM USE, 16 September. The arrows next to the name and the company on the outreach card do NOT go to LinkedIn. Oliver has to open the person in Lovable first and jump from there, which is a step and a page load on every card he reviews.

Three destinations, all reachable from the review card itself:
  The lead's Sales Navigator profile, next to their name. That is where the message is actually sent from.
  Their plain LinkedIn profile, on the lead view.
  The COMPANY WEBSITE, next to the company name.

The website matters for the same reason as the profile: the review decision often needs a ten second look at the site. Does the checkout still show what the guardrail claims. Do they actually sell the device. Is the incumbent still named on the product page. Today that means leaving the card, opening the company, and finding the URL.

Make the website clickable on the company record too. It is currently a plain text field there.

Raised to must. It was a nice to have at one extra click; at review volume it is a step on every card, and the review queue is already the bottleneck.$il$,
$il$THE OPEN QUESTION IN THIS ITEM IS NOW ANSWERED. It previously said: there are already arrow icons next to the name and the company, worth confirming what they open before building a second one. Oliver has confirmed they do not reach LinkedIn. So either repoint those arrows or add the buttons, but do not end up with two arrows doing different things.

SALES NAV, NOT THE PLAIN PROFILE, on the outreach card. The standing rule is that Sales Nav is the default channel even for first degree connections, so the sales lead record is the working destination and the /in/ profile is the fallback.

EVERY ONE OF THESE WILL BE DEAD FOR SOME RECORDS, so handle it the same way in all three cases. 242 of 665 contacts have no Sales Nav URL and 119 have neither LinkedIn URL. The website field is empty on a large share of companies too. Fallback order for the person: Sales Nav URL, then the plain LinkedIn URL, then a Sales Nav people search on name and company, then grey the button out and say no link on file. For the company: the website, then nothing, greyed out with the same message. A greyed button that explains itself is a prompt to fix the record; a dead button that does nothing is just broken.

OPEN IN A NEW TAB, per i035, with rel noopener noreferrer. Oliver works across tabs, and losing the card to a navigation is the opposite of saving a step.

NOT IN CONFLICT WITH i019. That parks automated outbound LinkedIn deep links in a scripted flow at volume. A human clicking through from a CRM to one profile is ordinary behaviour and every CRM does it. Different thing, and the caution should not block this.$il$);
